      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Reflex sympathetic dystrophy (RSD) is a poorly understood syndrome of post-traumatic pain, autonomic dysfunction, and progressive tissue atrophy. Classical descriptions of the cutaneous manifestations of RSD are usually limited to skin atrophy, vascular instability, and hyperhidrosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Our objective was to further delineate the cutaneous changes in RSD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We have observed RSD-related inflammatory and bullous lesions in nine patients with active RSD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Eight patients had significant edema of involved skin, two patients had evidence of a pigmented purpura-like inflammatory dermatitis, and two other patients had bullae on involved skin. Ultrastructural studies on a biopsy specimen from one patient with recurrent bullae revealed a disrupted basement membrane and abnormal anchoring fibrils."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Skin disease in RSD is more diverse than commonly appreciated and includes severe edema, inflammatory lesions, and a nonimmune bullous eruption."
        }
      ]},
